{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788296285,"nonce":"MQlDaJN4HSnntlz4b9meZYddvjA=","title":"Session 21 — the M1 gate, all four items, fanned out across four worktrees","message":"Session 21 — the M1 gate, all four items, and the round is not done until five things are green on a live bench.\n\nFanned out on the criticism session 20 earned for running solo. Four opus workers, four git worktrees, four branches off `c429dda`. The orchestrator launches everything; **workers never launch anything.**\n\n## The five deliverables\n\n1. **Acceptance for `61794cd` + `40ed42f`.** Nothing else can be trusted until it exists — both shipped in session 20 and neither has met a bench. New surface: `health.ticks_until_bleedout`, `health.bleedout{outcome, deathless_gene, prevents_death, game_shows_clock, blood_loss_severity}`, the `IsLethal` rank band, `digest.work_coverage`, `work-cover`, `triage`. Branch `spec/accept-bleed-triage`, worker A, `accept/` only.\n2. **`722c951`** — the unread-journal refusal AND the own-faction casualty halt. Grep confirmed neither exists. Plus `40ed42f` part 3 (refuse when the bleed clock is shorter than the rescuer's travel), which belongs on this machinery and CONSUMES `triage`'s numbers rather than growing a second path. Branch `spec/advance-halt`, worker B.\n3. **`b1b3060`** — the posture verb and `digest.posture`. Held last round; it is the fourth gate item. Branch `spec/posture`, worker C.\n4. **`7382bdd`** — promoted. Branch `spec/arg-names`, worker D.\n5. **The bench proof**, run by the orchestrator, raw envelopes banked under `accept/runs/`.\n\nNOT this session: the M1 re-run itself (`664e9b9`). Ship the gate, prove it, hand over.\n\n## The one contract decided from the chair rather than by a worker\n\n`722c951`'s escape hatches change every suite that advances, so their shape could not wait for worker B to choose it. Two per-call arguments on `advance`, each a REQUIRED non-empty reason string, each journalled as an act (session 13's `threat-pardon` precedent — the decision must be a recorded ACT, not a silent exemption):\n\n- `advance {unread_ok: \"\u003creason\u003e\"}` — proceed despite an unread journal delta.\n- `advance {through_casualties: \"\u003creason\u003e\"}` — do not halt on an own-faction downing/death, and do not refuse on a short bleed clock.\n\nWorkers A and C were given it up front so their suites arrive compatible instead of going red and being patched.\n\n## The steer on `7382bdd`, which is the one place this session reopens a settled decision\n\nSession 15 attempted the per-verb declared arg whitelist and rejected it on measurement — 120 verbs, 22 handlers that read no argument at their own call site, 88 `(verb,key)` pairs read through shared helpers, five suite call sites that build their dict at runtime. That measurement is sound and is not being re-litigated. But **every one of those objections is an objection to a second source of truth, and none of them is an objection to observing what the verb actually read.** `VerbArgs` is one sealed class over one dict and every accessor goes through it, so a read-log is derived from the code itself: zero declarations, zero drift, all 120 verbs. Worker D was steered there, told to measure the conditionally-read-argument risk honestly, and told the fallback (report-only `ignored_args`) is a complete fix for the silence and an acceptable outcome.\n\n## Ownership, because a shared HEAD is the hazard (session 4)\n\n- A: `accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.*`, `checklists/`, the doctor lesson. No source.\n- B: `Journal.cs`, `JournalVerbs.cs`, `TimeDriver.cs`, `TimeVerbs.cs`, `JournalHooks.cs`, and every other `accept/*` for the blast radius.\n- C: `SeekVerbs.cs`, `DigestVerb.cs`, the seek lesson, `accept/b1b3060-posture.*`.\n- D: `VerbRegistry.cs`, the dispatch seam, `JournalVerbs.Selftest`, the `Sub()` forwarding sites, `accept/s13-mod-surface.py`.\n\nNo branch carries an assembly. One standalone `Build:` on `main` after all four merges.","files":null}]}